WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.180
Welcome to the Deep Dive.

00:01.820 --> 00:03.980
Forget scrolling endlessly through stuff.

00:04.100 --> 00:08.420
We take the sources you choose and, you know, pull out the really important insight.

00:08.560 --> 00:10.540
Yeah, it's like a quick way to get properly informed.

00:10.720 --> 00:11.080
Exactly.

00:11.400 --> 00:15.460
Think of it as upgrading your understanding fast.

00:15.840 --> 00:22.340
And today we're diving into a really fascinating, maybe slightly mind-bending idea, ontological

00:22.340 --> 00:23.820
polycompilation.

00:24.220 --> 00:24.620
That's right.

00:24.680 --> 00:26.900
We've got a couple of really interesting sources from our listener.

00:26.900 --> 00:32.080
There's this academic paper, it's called Ontological Polycompilation, a constructor

00:32.080 --> 00:35.780
theory extension for substrate symbol emergence and knowledge creation.

00:36.080 --> 00:36.840
Okay, quite a title.

00:37.040 --> 00:37.220
Yeah.

00:37.360 --> 00:41.680
And alongside that, David Deutsch's lecture, Knowledge Creation and its Risks.

00:42.160 --> 00:43.900
So two pretty foundational pieces.

00:44.180 --> 00:44.440
Right.

00:44.580 --> 00:48.240
So our mission then is to try and get our heads around this new theory.

00:48.620 --> 00:53.400
What's it saying about how knowledge, how complex behaviors actually, you know, arise?

00:53.400 --> 00:57.460
And what does it mean for us, for how we think, maybe even for future tech?

00:57.840 --> 00:58.240
Exactly.

00:58.400 --> 01:00.380
What could it mean for AI and things like that?

01:00.920 --> 01:03.720
Hopefully we'll have some light bulb moments for you.

01:03.940 --> 01:04.220
Okay.

01:05.000 --> 01:06.320
Ontological polycompilation.

01:07.080 --> 01:08.000
It's a mouthful.

01:09.060 --> 01:10.880
Where do we even start with this?

01:11.180 --> 01:14.720
Well, I think it's best to start with the foundations it builds on.

01:14.720 --> 01:17.880
So the paper draws heavily on constructor theory.

01:18.180 --> 01:18.860
Constructor theory, right.

01:19.020 --> 01:25.620
Which is basically a framework for understanding the universe, but not just in terms of what

01:25.620 --> 01:27.260
is happening, but what can happen.

01:27.340 --> 01:27.500
Yeah.

01:27.600 --> 01:30.600
What transformations are physically possible and which ones are impossible.

01:31.000 --> 01:34.220
So it's about the potential, the rules of the game almost.

01:34.500 --> 01:35.100
Kind of, yeah.

01:35.180 --> 01:37.500
The ultimate rule book for physical transformations.

01:37.840 --> 01:38.720
And then you have David Deutsch.

01:38.940 --> 01:39.160
Correct.

01:39.160 --> 01:43.420
His lecture focuses on knowledge creation as this uniquely human thing, this incredibly

01:43.420 --> 01:44.840
powerful capability we have.

01:45.060 --> 01:48.140
But he also adds a note of caution, doesn't he, about the risks involved.

01:48.320 --> 01:48.800
Precisely.

01:49.060 --> 01:54.020
He argues that because knowledge creation is so open-ended, we can, you know, stumble into

01:54.020 --> 01:55.420
problems we didn't foresee.

01:55.740 --> 02:00.780
We might create systems or ideas that don't actually serve us well, or we misunderstand them.

02:00.780 --> 02:00.960
Right.

02:01.160 --> 02:05.000
And what's really interesting here, what Ontological Polycompilation tries to do is connect these

02:05.000 --> 02:06.140
two big ideas.

02:06.140 --> 02:11.120
It connects the fundamental possibilities from constructor theory.

02:11.540 --> 02:14.660
With the power and potential pitfalls of knowledge creation.

02:14.820 --> 02:16.000
To explain something really basic.

02:16.160 --> 02:16.300
Yeah.

02:16.380 --> 02:21.040
How does physical stuff, like, you know, matter and energy, become meaningful symbols in our

02:21.040 --> 02:21.380
heads?

02:21.820 --> 02:26.720
And how does that process drive the creation of new knowledge?

02:27.080 --> 02:27.280
Okay.

02:27.320 --> 02:28.060
So that's the bridge.

02:28.100 --> 02:30.880
How do we go from the physical world to abstract thought?

02:31.100 --> 02:33.500
That's where the Polycompilation bit comes in, I guess.

02:33.560 --> 02:33.960
Exactly.

02:33.960 --> 02:37.520
The theory introduces a few key concepts.

02:37.780 --> 02:39.580
First, you've got substrates.

02:40.020 --> 02:40.460
Substrates.

02:40.640 --> 02:41.140
Physical things.

02:41.160 --> 02:42.200
Yeah, the actual physical stuff.

02:42.280 --> 02:46.420
Could be your brain cells, silicon in a chip, even a simple stone tool.

02:46.580 --> 02:46.840
Okay.

02:47.000 --> 02:47.860
Then you have symbols.

02:48.240 --> 02:51.880
These are the representations, the meanings we associate with those physical substrates.

02:52.000 --> 02:56.940
So like a pattern of neurons firing could be the symbol for dog, or bits in a computer

02:56.940 --> 02:57.980
could symbolize a word.

02:58.300 --> 02:59.140
Exactly that.

02:59.440 --> 03:01.420
Physical stuff and the meanings linked to it.

03:01.740 --> 03:02.220
Got it.

03:02.220 --> 03:05.220
Now, what about this constructor idea from earlier?

03:05.340 --> 03:06.100
How does that fit?

03:06.500 --> 03:06.600
Okay.

03:06.680 --> 03:11.440
So a constructor, in this context, is something that can perform a transformation on a substrate,

03:11.840 --> 03:17.680
but it doesn't get used up doing it, like a catalyst in chemistry or maybe a 3D printer

03:17.680 --> 03:18.720
following a design.

03:18.880 --> 03:19.100
Okay.

03:19.220 --> 03:21.260
It enables a change without being consumed.

03:21.640 --> 03:21.960
Right.

03:22.340 --> 03:27.080
But then the paper introduces something really crucial, the polyconstructor.

03:27.480 --> 03:28.040
Polyconstructor.

03:28.160 --> 03:29.240
So more than one?

03:29.240 --> 03:32.000
Well, it's more about being dynamic and evolving.

03:32.520 --> 03:35.180
Think of a regular constructor as maybe a single purpose tool.

03:35.640 --> 03:39.660
A polyconstructor is more like a system that can learn and adapt.

03:39.940 --> 03:40.280
Adapt how?

03:40.620 --> 03:46.120
Crucially, it can repurpose substrates those physical things into new symbolic meanings,

03:46.220 --> 03:46.740
new domains.

03:46.960 --> 03:48.400
And it does this through feedback loops.

03:48.800 --> 03:49.480
It's recursive.

03:49.660 --> 03:50.140
Recursive.

03:50.240 --> 03:50.420
Yeah.

03:50.520 --> 03:52.080
Meaning it feeds back on itself.

03:52.180 --> 03:53.480
It learns from what it just did.

03:53.560 --> 03:53.900
Precisely.

03:53.900 --> 03:57.900
It's a constructor that doesn't just, you know, do the transformation, it also learns

03:57.900 --> 03:58.560
from the outcome.

03:58.820 --> 04:03.180
And that changes how it interacts with things next time, even how it represents them symbolically.

04:03.420 --> 04:03.440
Okay.

04:03.500 --> 04:04.340
That sounds powerful.

04:04.680 --> 04:08.500
It leads them to define the reappropriation operator, which they call R.

04:08.800 --> 04:09.620
Ah, okay.

04:09.820 --> 04:15.580
This R is the actual process, the mechanism of mapping a physical substrate to a symbol,

04:16.660 --> 04:18.960
taking the physical thing and assigning it a meaning.

04:19.060 --> 04:22.080
Like turning the sensation of heat into the idea of hot.

04:22.080 --> 04:23.280
Sort of, yeah.

04:23.820 --> 04:27.580
But the really key point is that this R, this mapping process, isn't fixed.

04:27.980 --> 04:30.780
It evolves as the system learns and experiences more.

04:30.980 --> 04:31.600
Whoa, okay.

04:31.680 --> 04:36.000
So the very way we turn reality into symbols can change based on our learning.

04:36.180 --> 04:36.660
Exactly.

04:36.900 --> 04:37.100
Yeah.

04:37.240 --> 04:38.560
And this gets to their main idea.

04:39.220 --> 04:40.560
Knowledge is polycompilation.

04:41.120 --> 04:44.240
They're saying that creating knowledge isn't just about gathering facts.

04:44.420 --> 04:49.260
It's about these polyconstructors repeatedly, recursively applying this R operator.

04:49.260 --> 04:53.580
Taking substrates and turning them into symbols and then maybe taking those symbols as new inputs.

04:53.740 --> 04:53.940
Yes.

04:54.600 --> 04:58.320
Reappropriating substrates into symbolic representations, layer by layer.

04:59.280 --> 05:04.180
Think about how we build complex concepts like, I don't know, justice or scientific method.

05:04.540 --> 05:11.140
We start with basic experiences, maybe some simple words, and we build up reappropriating previous symbols into more abstract ones.

05:11.140 --> 05:14.420
Okay, so this R operator gets used over and over.

05:14.620 --> 05:16.020
The paper has that formula, right?

05:16.400 --> 05:17.040
R and S.

05:17.240 --> 05:18.500
R, R, N, 1.

05:18.760 --> 05:21.380
Looks technical, but the idea is repeated application.

05:21.720 --> 05:22.540
That's exactly it.

05:22.680 --> 05:27.200
R applied once, R1S, gives you a basic symbol from the substrate S.

05:27.420 --> 05:28.920
Then you take that symbol as the input.

05:29.180 --> 05:31.080
And apply R again, R2S.

05:31.160 --> 05:31.320
Right.

05:31.440 --> 05:32.660
And you get a higher level symbol.

05:32.760 --> 05:37.180
Maybe a symbol representing a relationship between other symbols or more abstract concept.

05:37.700 --> 05:39.640
This is how they see complexity emerging.

05:39.740 --> 05:40.160
Makes sense.

05:40.160 --> 05:42.400
They also mention category theory briefly.

05:42.800 --> 05:42.880
Yeah.

05:43.040 --> 05:44.820
They touch on that just, you know, at a high level.

05:44.940 --> 05:46.420
It's a mathematical way to formalize this.

05:46.660 --> 05:49.840
Think of substrates and symbols as objects in a network.

05:50.260 --> 05:55.780
And applying R is like drawing the arrows, the transformations that build up these hierarchies of meaning.

05:56.020 --> 05:56.280
Okay.

05:56.600 --> 06:01.220
And what about these fixed points they talk about where R, S actually equals S?

06:01.380 --> 06:03.500
The symbol doesn't change when R is applied.

06:03.820 --> 06:04.520
Those are interesting.

06:04.520 --> 06:09.440
They're seen as really stable symbols or concepts.

06:09.440 --> 06:13.400
Maybe like a fundamental law of physics or a core mathematical idea.

06:13.580 --> 06:17.480
Something that's been reappropriated and tested so much has become robust.

06:17.600 --> 06:20.060
It represents solid, established knowledge.

06:20.180 --> 06:20.480
Right.

06:20.580 --> 06:24.160
It's stable because it works consistently across many contexts.

06:24.160 --> 06:27.780
They also propose this polycompilation axiom.

06:27.980 --> 06:28.300
Oh, yeah.

06:28.380 --> 06:35.760
Which says that any constructor that can use this R operator can lead to an even more powerful polyconstructor.

06:36.240 --> 06:39.200
That sounds like an engine for ever-increasing complexity.

06:39.480 --> 06:40.800
That's definitely the implication.

06:41.020 --> 06:46.900
It suggests that once you have this basic substrate-to-symbol mapping and the ability for that mapping to adapt.

06:46.900 --> 06:52.980
Then you almost inevitably get more sophisticated systems emerging, systems capable of creating more complex knowledge.

06:53.100 --> 06:54.180
That seems to be the idea.

06:54.420 --> 06:56.000
And then there's the symbol emergence condition.

06:56.140 --> 06:56.400
Okay.

06:56.620 --> 07:04.320
Which basically says a symbol is truly embodied, properly integrated, and meaningful only if it's the result of this recursive process.

07:04.600 --> 07:08.320
If R has been applied more than once, R and S, where N is greater than 1.

07:08.320 --> 07:16.000
And is this where the connection back to Deutsch's risks comes in, if you just keep applying R over and over, R and S, with a really big N?

07:16.120 --> 07:16.560
Exactly.

07:17.020 --> 07:22.960
You could get these long chains of symbols built on symbols, potentially losing touch with the original physical substrate.

07:23.080 --> 07:24.000
Runaway symbol creation.

07:24.360 --> 07:25.600
That's the risk they acknowledge.

07:26.480 --> 07:34.240
This powerful recursive engine for knowledge also carries the danger of uncontrolled bootstrapping.

07:34.240 --> 07:41.380
You could end up with these very elaborate symbolic castles in the air that don't actually map onto anything useful or real.

07:41.660 --> 07:46.320
Which could lead to the kinds of misunderstandings or misaligned AI that Deutsch warns about.

07:46.640 --> 07:47.120
Precisely.

07:47.400 --> 07:48.740
Okay, let's make this more concrete.

07:49.280 --> 07:51.320
The paper uses tool use as an example.

07:51.900 --> 07:55.580
How does polycompilation explain something like, say, using a stone tool?

07:55.760 --> 07:55.980
Right.

07:56.060 --> 07:56.620
So they break down.

07:56.720 --> 07:59.400
First, you have the hand that's your initial substrate, S1.

07:59.740 --> 08:04.020
The act of grasping and manipulating is the first reappropriation, R1.

08:04.600 --> 08:08.260
This leads to a new capability, maybe a cutting motion if you grasp something sharp.

08:08.460 --> 08:08.600
Okay.

08:08.720 --> 08:10.660
Basic physical action and its immediate outcome.

08:11.060 --> 08:16.160
Then they say, consider the hand plus the stone tool together as a new substrate, S2.

08:16.820 --> 08:19.460
Now, the reappropriation isn't just the physical cutting.

08:19.840 --> 08:20.540
It expands.

08:20.780 --> 08:21.400
Expands how?

08:21.520 --> 08:25.040
Maybe it includes gestures you make with the tool, communicating intent.

08:25.300 --> 08:28.700
Those gestures can then be reappropriated into icons, into signals.

08:28.700 --> 08:34.500
Which could eventually lead to changes in the brain, cortical remapping, maybe laying groundwork for language.

08:34.680 --> 08:36.140
That's the kind of chain they're suggesting.

08:36.340 --> 08:46.220
A simple physical interaction kicks off a cascade of reappropriations, RNS, leading to increasingly abstract representations and abilities, like communication.

08:46.580 --> 08:46.920
Wow.

08:46.920 --> 08:51.040
But this chain, while creating knowledge, could also get stuck, right?

08:51.340 --> 08:52.260
Create rigid symbols.

08:52.520 --> 08:55.420
Like, this is the only way to hold this tool, even if it's inefficient.

08:55.740 --> 08:56.140
Exactly.

08:56.440 --> 08:57.720
That illustrates the tension.

08:58.200 --> 08:59.920
You need the symbols, the abstractions.

08:59.920 --> 09:06.420
But there's a risk they become detached or overly rigid if that reappropriation, R, isn't somehow grounded or adaptable.

09:06.720 --> 09:14.380
This feels like it links really strongly to embodied cognition, the idea that thinking isn't just abstract brain stuff, but tied to our bodies and actions.

09:14.860 --> 09:15.280
Absolutely.

09:16.060 --> 09:19.740
Poly compilation offers a potential mechanism for how that happens.

09:19.820 --> 09:24.980
How our sensorimotor loops, the physical interactions, the substrates get reappropriated into symbols.

09:25.100 --> 09:26.760
Symbols that then guide our next actions.

09:26.940 --> 09:27.060
Right.

09:27.060 --> 09:30.640
Think about learning a skill, like riding a bike.

09:31.200 --> 09:32.700
Initially, it's all physical fumbling.

09:33.360 --> 09:38.300
But gradually, you develop an internal symbolic sense of balance of timing.

09:38.560 --> 09:40.360
That's reappropriation at work.

09:40.660 --> 09:47.600
And the recursive part, RNS, lets us build more abstract ideas on top of that embodied experience, learning and adapting.

09:48.060 --> 09:48.500
Exactly.

09:48.880 --> 09:53.580
And what's even more interesting, maybe, is the idea that systems can reappropriate their own symbols.

09:53.800 --> 09:54.300
Turning inwards.

09:54.300 --> 09:54.640
Yeah.

09:55.060 --> 09:57.060
The symbols themselves become the new substrate.

09:57.680 --> 10:04.240
This could be the basis for metacognition, thinking about your own thinking, reflecting on your concepts, improving them, self-awareness, potentially.

10:04.620 --> 10:08.380
So our internal symbolic world becomes a substrate for further reappropriation.

10:08.600 --> 10:09.460
Okay, that's pretty deep.

10:09.840 --> 10:13.160
How does this all neatly tie back into constructor theory's big picture?

10:13.160 --> 10:21.560
Well, polycompilation views constructors, those fundamental transformers of substrates, as the things that actually apply this reappropriation operator are.

10:21.880 --> 10:25.140
So constructors are the agents doing the symbolic mapping.

10:25.620 --> 10:26.060
Yes.

10:26.060 --> 10:32.740
And crucially, it suggests polyconstructors can recursively build new constructors.

10:33.160 --> 10:36.140
They can create new ways of transforming things, new functionalities.

10:36.400 --> 10:42.880
Ah, so it's not just mapping symbols, it's creating new capabilities within the bounds of what constructor theory says is possible.

10:43.100 --> 10:43.460
Exactly.

10:43.940 --> 10:50.160
It provides a mechanism for exploring and expanding those possibilities through generating new knowledge and new constructors.

10:50.160 --> 10:55.880
Okay. Now, the paper anticipates some pushback. What about the substrate neutrality objection?

10:56.480 --> 11:00.720
The idea that computation is the same regardless of the hardware, silicon, neurons, whatever.

11:01.180 --> 11:02.940
Why focus so much on the substrate?

11:03.560 --> 11:06.040
Yeah, that's a common argument in, like, philosophy of mine.

11:06.400 --> 11:07.580
The paper's response is interesting.

11:07.700 --> 11:15.360
They say, okay, maybe the abstract logic of a computation could be neutral, but the emergence of meaning, the interpretive layers...

11:15.360 --> 11:16.800
And you actually understand the output.

11:16.800 --> 11:19.880
Right. That, they argue, is substrate dependent.

11:20.160 --> 11:27.120
It arises through this specific process of polycompilation, of R acting on that particular substrate.

11:27.480 --> 11:33.180
This actually aligns with Deutsch's view that knowledge isn't purely abstract, it's tied to physical reality.

11:33.660 --> 11:37.240
So the way meaning gets built depends on the physical foundation it's built upon.

11:37.560 --> 11:41.800
Okay. What about the other objection that it ignores Deutsch's risks?

11:42.320 --> 11:45.720
Does polycompilation just focus on the creation part and forget the danger?

11:46.000 --> 11:47.360
No, they address that too.

11:47.360 --> 11:53.460
Their counter is that the framework actually models these risks, those uncontrolled RNS chains we talked about.

11:53.540 --> 11:54.320
The runaway symbols.

11:54.580 --> 11:56.840
That is their formal representation of the risk.

11:57.180 --> 11:58.020
It's not an oversight.

11:58.340 --> 11:59.720
It's an outcome the model can describe.

11:59.980 --> 12:04.440
And the proposed solution isn't to stop R, but to figure out how to put constraints on R.

12:04.640 --> 12:08.380
Constraints to keep the symbols grounded, interpretable, useful.

12:08.380 --> 12:14.300
Exactly. How do we ensure the reappropriation process doesn't spiral off into dangerous or meaningless territory?

12:14.800 --> 12:17.780
That becomes a key research question, especially for AI safety.

12:18.200 --> 12:19.940
Which leads nicely into future research.

12:20.120 --> 12:22.340
The paper maps out some next steps, doesn't it?

12:22.620 --> 12:23.140
It does.

12:23.860 --> 12:27.980
One really intriguing idea is this polycompilative density function.

12:28.100 --> 12:30.020
Sounds complex. What's the goal?

12:30.020 --> 12:44.940
It's an attempt to quantify how efficiently a given substrate can be turned into unique symbols, like how much meaning-making potential does this chunk of matter or energy have per unit, measuring its richness for polycompilation.

12:45.160 --> 12:46.840
Huh. Interesting. What else?

12:47.020 --> 12:56.720
Well, they also highlight needing to model symbol bootstrapping, like what's the minimum N in RNS for a truly meaningful symbol to emerge? How does it get started?

12:56.720 --> 13:04.620
And those constraints on R we just mentioned, how to build systems where R doesn't produce, say, uninterpretable AI outputs or harmful ideologies.

13:04.640 --> 13:09.840
Exactly. That's crucial. And they really stress the need for empirical validation. This isn't just armchair theorizing.

13:09.960 --> 13:17.520
Right. They want to see if you can actually observe this recursive reappropriation happening in, say, brains or maybe even in evolving AI systems.

13:17.620 --> 13:22.040
Precisely. They suggest using computational models to simulate R, see what emerges.

13:22.040 --> 13:28.460
And they see huge potential for cross-disciplinary work linking up neuroscience, robotics, linguistics, AI.

13:28.700 --> 13:30.760
Using polycompilation as a shared framework.

13:31.100 --> 13:38.040
Potentially, yeah. Offering new ways to think about intelligence, learning, and how to build better, safer artificial systems.

13:38.160 --> 13:46.760
Okay. So wrapping this up, ontological polycompilation. It's a pretty ambitious framework. It's trying to explain how complexity and knowledge itself emerge.

13:46.760 --> 13:57.080
Yeah. By proposing this core mechanism, recursive reappropriation, turning physical stuff into symbols, then symbols into more symbols, building layers of meaning.

13:57.360 --> 14:07.640
And it does this by extending constructor theory's focus on possibility while also directly engaging with David Deutsch's ideas about knowledge creation and, importantly, its risks.

14:07.640 --> 14:15.780
Exactly. It tries to provide the how for knowledge creation within what's possible of physics, acknowledging the dangers along the way.

14:15.980 --> 14:23.760
It feels like it could genuinely offer new perspectives, connecting fields that don't always talk to each other easily. Cognition, AI, maybe even evolution.

14:24.160 --> 14:32.900
That seems to be the hope. A potential unifying lens to look at how complex, adaptive knowledge-creating systems come about, whether they're biological or artificial.

14:32.900 --> 14:40.780
This has been definitely a deep dive. Really makes you think about the hidden layers of abstraction that underpin everything we think and do.

14:41.300 --> 14:52.340
So, a final thought for you, our listener. Consider this framework. What are the substrates in your own life, your daily experiences, the news you read, the skills you practice?

14:52.900 --> 15:00.120
And how are they being constantly reappropriated by your own internal R operator into new symbols, new understandings, new connections?

15:00.120 --> 15:04.040
What new knowledge is that recursive process generating within you right now?

15:04.140 --> 15:09.140
And maybe following Deutsch in this paper, are there any risks in how those symbols are forming? Something to mull over.

15:09.740 --> 15:11.420
Thanks for joining us on the Deep Dive.

